Implement a bulk `setPriorities` in this p-queue workspace (v10.0.0). All facts below come from the completed repo survey; do not search the tree for these paths/signatures.

FILES TO EDIT (production):
1. `source/queue.ts`
2. `source/priority-queue.ts`
3. `source/index.ts`

NEW TEST FILE (create directory if needed):
4. `benchmark_tests/set-priorities.ts`

IMMUTABLE — do NOT edit: `test/*`, `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `bench.ts`, `.bench/`, `dist/`.

Public signature to add everywhere: `setPriorities(updates: ReadonlyArray<{id: string; priority: number}>): void`

------------------------------------------------------------
### 1. source/queue.ts
Current full file is:
```
export type RunFunction = () => Promise<unknown>;

export type Queue<Element, Options> = {
    size: number;
    filter: (options: Readonly<Partial<Options>>) => Element[];
    dequeue: () => Element | undefined;
    enqueue: (run: Element, options?: Partial<Options>) => void;
    setPriority: (id: string, priority: number) => void;
    remove: (id: string) => void;
};
```
Add one OPTIONAL member (so existing custom queue types without it still satisfy `Queue`):
```
    setPriorities?: (updates: ReadonlyArray<{
        id: string;
        priority: number;
    }>) => void;
```
Keep tabs / no semicolons (sindresorhus style).

------------------------------------------------------------
### 2. source/priority-queue.ts
`export default class PriorityQueue implements Queue<RunFunction, PriorityQueueOptions>` (around line 11). Its existing `setPriority(id, priority)` (around lines 92-105) throws `ReferenceError(\`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.\`)` when `this.#findIndex(element => element.id === id)` is `-1`, otherwise splices the item and re-enqueues (reusing FIFO tie-breaking in `enqueue`).

Add a new `setPriorities(updates: ReadonlyArray<{id: string; priority: number}>)` next to `setPriority`:
- Validate the ENTIRE batch before any mutation, in this order:
  1. For each update, if `typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)` throw `TypeError(\`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})\`)`.
  2. For each update, if `this.#findIndex(element => element.id === id) === -1` throw `ReferenceError(\`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.\`)`.
- Then apply in array order by calling the existing `this.setPriority(id, priority)` for each update. This preserves single-update ordering and makes duplicate ids resolve to the last value (last wins).
- Empty array is naturally a no-op (loops do nothing).
- Match existing formatting (tabs, no semicolons).

------------------------------------------------------------
### 3. source/index.ts
The `PQueue` class starts around line 97. Existing single method (lines 837-852):
```
setPriority(id: string, priority: number) {
    if (typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)) {
        throw new TypeError(`Expected \`priority\` to be a finite number, got \`${priority}\` (${typeof priority})`);
    }
    // Checked here, not only in `PriorityQueue`, so that it also throws with a custom queue class.
    for (const waitingTask of this.#waitingTasks) {
        if (waitingTask.id === id) {
            this.#queue.setPriority(id, priority);
            waitingTask.priority = priority;
            return;
        }
    }
    throw new ReferenceError(`No promise function with the id "${id}" exists in the queue.`);
}
```
`WaitingTask` (lines 81-90) has `id?: string` and `priority?: number`; `#waitingTasks` is a `Set<WaitingTask>` (line 154). A running task is removed from `#waitingTasks` when it starts (run wrapper at lines 641-644), so an already-running id is simply absent from `#waitingTasks` (same `ReferenceError` path as unknown).

Add a public `setPriorities(updates: ReadonlyArray<{id: string; priority: number}>)` method next to `setPriority`, with a JSDoc comment mirroring the existing `setPriority` JSDoc style. Exact behavior:

1. Validate the ENTIRE batch BEFORE any mutation:
   - For each update: if `typeof priority !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(priority)` throw the same `TypeError` message above.
   - For each update: if no `waitingTask` in `#waitingTasks` has `waitingTask.id === id`, throw the same `ReferenceError` message above. (This covers unknown AND already-running ids.)
2. If `updates.length === 0` return (no-op).
3. Apply:
   - If `this.#queue.setPriorities` is defined: call `this.#queue.setPriorities(updates)`, then loop `updates` in order and set the matching `WaitingTask.priority = priority` (later occurrences overwrite, so duplicate ids resolve last-wins).
   - Else (custom queue lacks the bulk method): loop `updates` in order and call the existing `this.setPriority(id, priority)` for each update — this is the validated single-update fallback and keeps both queue ordering and `WaitingTask.priority` consistent.
   - Use `waitingTask.id === id` first-match semantics identical to `setPriority`. Do NOT change the existing `setPriority` behavior.

Implementation note: you may add a small private helper to find a waiting task by id, or inline the search. Match existing tabs / no semicolons / `.js` import-extension style.

